Here is a simple C++ program that takes an input from the user and prints out the corresponding Fibonacci number:
```c++
#include <iostream>

int fibonacci(int n) {
  if (n <= 1) {
    return n;
  } else {
    return fibonacci(n-1) + fibonacci(n-2);
  }
}

int main() {
  int n;
  std::cout << "Enter a number: ";
  std::cin >> n;
  std::cout << "The corresponding Fibonacci number is " << fibonacci(n) << std::endl;
  return 0;
}
```

This program defines a recursive function `fibonacci` that takes an integer `n` as input and returns the `n`th Fibonacci number. The `main` function prompts the user for an input, calls the `fibonacci` function to get the corresponding Fibonacci number, and then prints it out.

Here is an example of a React component that animates a text property as the property changes, simulating a keyboard typing effect:
```
import React, { useState } from 'react';

const TypingText = () => {
  const [text, setText] = useState('');

  const type = (char) => {
    if (text.length < 100) {
      setText(text + char);
      setTimeout(() => type(char), 100);
    }
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <p>{text}</p>
      <button onClick={() => type('a')}>Type 'a'</button>
      <button onClick={() => type('b')}>Type 'b'</button>
      <button onClick={() => type('c')}>Type 'c'</button>
    </div>
  );
};

export default TypingText;
```

This component has a `text` state variable that is used to store the current text value. The `type` function is called whenever a button is clicked, and it adds the specified character to the `text` value and schedules another call to `type` after a 100 milliseconds delay. This creates the illusion of characters being typed one at a time.
You can customize the behavior of the typing text by modifying the `type` function. For example, you could add more characters to the text as it is typed, or you could change the delay between characters. You could also use the `text` state variable to store more complex data structures and animate the display of that data in a similar way.

Here is some code you could use in your HTTP middleware to set the error on Datadog traces based on the Twirp error instead of a generic internal server error:

```go
import (
  "github.com/twitchtv/twirp"
  "gopkg.in/DataDog/dd-trace-go.v1/ddtrace/tracer"
)

func middleware(next http.Handler) http.Handler {
  return http.HandlerFunc(func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
    span := tracer.StartSpan("request", tracer.ResourceName("/some/endpoint"))
    defer span.Finish()

    err := next.ServeHTTP(w, r)

    // Check if error is a Twirp error
    if twerr, ok := err.(twirp.Error); ok {
      // Set error on span
      span.SetTag("error", true)
      span.SetTag("error.msg", twerr.Msg())
      span.SetTag("error.type", twerr.Error())
    } else if err != nil {
      // Handle other errors
    }
  })
}
```

The key points:

- Use `tracer.StartSpan` to start a span for the request 
- In the deferred `Finish`, check if error is a Twirp error
- If yes, set error tags on span using the Twirp error message and type 
- If no, handle other errors

This will allow Datadog to show the actual Twirp error in traces instead of a generic 500 error.

