Sure! Here is an example of how you could create a React component that displays a number and increments it every time the button is clicked:
```javascript
import React, { useState } from 'react';

function Counter() {
  // Declare a state variable called "count" and initialize it to 0
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  // Define a function called "increment" that increments the "count" state variable by 1
  const increment = () => {
    setCount(count + 1);
  };

  // Render the button and the current count
  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={increment}>Increment</button>
      <p>Count: {count}</p>
    </div>
  );
}

export default Counter;
```

To use this component in your application, you would import it and render it like this:
```javascript
import React from 'react';
import Counter from './Counter';

function App() {
  return (
    <div>
      <Counter />
    </div>
  );
}

export default App;
```

Every time the button is clicked, the count will be incremented and displayed on the page.

