Sure! Here is an example of a Java 17 application that consumes messages from an Azure Event Hub topic:
```java
import reactor.core.publisher.Flux;
import reactor.core.publisher.Mono;
import reactor.core.publisher.MonoSource;
import reactor.core.publisher.BroadcastEmitter;
import reactor.core.publisher.FluxSink;

import java.time.Duration;
import java.time.Instant;
import java.util.concurrent.CompletionStage;

public class EventHubConsumer {
    private final String connectionString;
    private final String topicName;
    private final String subscriptionName;

    public EventHubConsumer(String connectionString, String topicName, String subscriptionName) {
        this.connectionString = connectionString;
        this.topicName = topicName;
        this.subscriptionName = subscriptionName;
    }

    public Mono<Void> consumeMessages() {
        returnMonoSource()
                .fromCallable(() -> {
                    EventHubClient client = EventHubClientBuilder.fromConnectionString(connectionString).build();

                    return client.createConsumer(topicName, subscriptionName)
                            .map(event -> {
                                System.out.println("Received event: " + event);
                                return null;
                            })
                            .doOnCancel(() -> client.close())
                            .doOnComplete(() -> {
                                System.out.println("Finished consuming messages");
                            })
                            .subscribe();
                })
                .then(Mono::empty);
    }
}
```

This code uses the `Reactor` library to create a `Mono` that consumes messages from the specified Event Hub topic. The `fromCallable` method creates a `Mono` that calls a lambda function to create an `EventHubConsumer` and consume messages from the topic. The `map` method then processes each message received and returns it. The `doOnCancel` and `doOnComplete` methods handle the cancellation and completion of the operation.
Note that this code assumes that you have already configured a connection string for your Event Hub, as well as the topic and subscription names. You can obtain these values by navigating to the Azure portal and looking at the Event Hub connection string.

